.TH "BASH\-SERVER\-CLIENT\-GO" "7" "2026\-02\-01" "bash\-server 0.1.0" "bash\-server concepts"
.SH DESCRIPTION
The Go client binding provides a goroutine\-based, concurrent interface
to the bash\-server v2 NDJSON protocol.
It is implemented as the \f[CR]bashclient\f[R] Go package, requires Go
1.21 or later, and depends only on the Go standard library
(\f[CR]net\f[R], \f[CR]os/exec\f[R], \f[CR]encoding/json\f[R],
\f[CR]bufio\f[R]).
.PP
The binding runs a background goroutine that reads NDJSON lines from the
transport and dispatches them to per\-channel response channels and
registered callback functions.
All request/response operations accept a \f[CR]context.Context\f[R] for
cancellation and timeout control.
.SH CONCEPTS
.SH Installation
The package is located at \f[CR]bash\-server/clients/go/\f[R] and uses
Go modules:
.IP
.EX
cd bash\-server/clients/go
go build ./...
.EE
.PP
To run tests:
.IP
.EX
go test ./...
.EE
.PP
To use the package in another module:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]import\f[R] \(dqgithub.com/cygwin/bash\-server/clients/go/bashclient\(dq
.EE
.SH Package structure
.IP
.EX
bashclient/
    client.go        \-\- BashClient type, connect, close, auth
    channels.go      \-\- Six channel types
    control.go       \-\- ControlChannel (channel 0)
    command.go       \-\- CommandChannel (channel 1)
    state.go         \-\- StateChannel (channel 2)
    observe.go       \-\- ObserveChannel (channel 3)
    debug.go         \-\- DebugChannel (channel 4)
    pty.go           \-\- PtyChannel (channel 5)
    errors.go        \-\- Error type hierarchy
    protocol.go      \-\- NDJSON encode/decode
    transport.go     \-\- Four transport implementations
    types.go         \-\- Exported type definitions
.EE
.SH Architecture
The central type is \f[CR]BashClient\f[R].
It owns a \f[CR]Transport\f[R] interface value and starts a background
reader goroutine at construction time.
.PP
The reader goroutine continuously reads NDJSON lines from the transport
and routes them:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Request/response messages\f[R] are sent on per\-channel
\f[CR]chan Message\f[R] instances.
When a channel method sends a request, it selects on the response
channel and the context\(cqs \f[CR]Done()\f[R] channel, blocking until a
response arrives or the context expires.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Server\-push messages\f[R] (observe events, debug break_hit, PTY
output/exit) are dispatched directly to registered callback functions
via the channel\(cqs \f[CR]dispatch()\f[R] method.
.PP
Each channel is exposed as a public field:
.IP
.EX
client.Control   \f[I]// *ControlChannel  (channel 0)\f[R]
client.Command   \f[I]// *CommandChannel  (channel 1)\f[R]
client.State     \f[I]// *StateChannel    (channel 2)\f[R]
client.Observe   \f[I]// *ObserveChannel  (channel 3)\f[R]
client.Debug     \f[I]// *DebugChannel    (channel 4)\f[R]
client.Pty       \f[I]// *PtyChannel      (channel 5)\f[R]
.EE
.SH Connection
\f[CR]bashclient\f[R] provides factory functions that return
\f[CR](*BashClient, error)\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// Unix domain socket (default)\f[R]
client, err := bashclient.Connect(ctx, \(dq/tmp/bash\-server\-1000/sock\(dq)

\f[I]// Subprocess (\-\-stdio mode)\f[R]
client, err := bashclient.ConnectStdio(ctx, \(dqbash\-server\(dq, \(dq\-\-stdio\(dq)

\f[I]// Inherited file descriptor\f[R]
client, err := bashclient.ConnectFd(ctx, 3)

\f[I]// Windows Named Pipe (Cygwin)\f[R]
client, err := bashclient.ConnectNamedPipe(ctx, \(ga\(rs\(rs.\(rspipe\(rsbash\-server\-myname\(ga)

\f[I]// From existing transport\f[R]
client := bashclient.FromTransport(myTransport)
.EE
.PP
All factory functions accept a \f[CR]context.Context\f[R] as the first
parameter.
The context controls the connection timeout but is not stored \(en
subsequent operations use their own contexts.
.PP
\f[CR]FromTransport()\f[R] takes an already\-connected transport and
immediately starts the reader goroutine.
It does not return an error.
.SH Lifecycle management
Go\(cqs \f[CR]defer\f[R] statement provides deterministic cleanup:
.IP
.EX
client, err := bashclient.Connect(ctx, socketPath)
\f[B]if\f[R] err != nil {
    log.Fatal(err)
}
\f[B]defer\f[R] client.Close()

err = client.Auth(ctx, token)
\f[B]if\f[R] err != nil {
    log.Fatal(err)
}
result, err := client.Eval(ctx, \(dqecho hello\(dq)
\f[B]if\f[R] err != nil {
    log.Fatal(err)
}
fmt.Println(result.Stdout)
.EE
.PP
The \f[CR]Close()\f[R] method:
.IP "1." 3
Signals the reader goroutine to stop via a \f[CR]done\f[R] channel.
.IP "2." 3
Sends a \f[CR]disconnect\f[R] message (ignoring errors).
.IP "3." 3
Closes the transport.
.IP "4." 3
Waits for the reader goroutine to exit.
.PP
\f[CR]Close()\f[R] is safe to call multiple times.
.SH Authentication
.IP
.EX
err := client.Auth(ctx, token)
.EE
.PP
The token is a 64\-character hex string.
Returns \f[CR]*AuthError\f[R] on failure.
Check authentication status:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]if\f[R] client.IsAuthenticated() {
    \f[I]// safe to use all channels\f[R]
}
.EE
