.SH Callback model
The observe, debug, and PTY channels use typed callback functions:
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// Register\f[R]
client.Observe.OnPreCommand(\f[B]func\f[R](e bashclient.PreCommandEvent) { ... })
client.Observe.OnPostCommand(\f[B]func\f[R](e bashclient.PostCommandEvent) { ... })
client.Debug.OnBreakHit(\f[B]func\f[R](e bashclient.BreakHitEvent) { ... })
client.Pty.OnOutput(\f[B]func\f[R](data string) { ... })
client.Pty.OnExit(\f[B]func\f[R](exitCode int) { ... })

\f[I]// Unregister (pass nil)\f[R]
client.Observe.OnPreCommand(nil)
.EE
.PP
Callbacks are invoked on the reader goroutine.
Only one callback per event type is active at a time.
Setting a new callback replaces the previous one.
Pass \f[CR]nil\f[R] to unregister.
.PP
Callbacks should be non\-blocking.
If heavy processing is needed, send the event to a separate goroutine
via a channel:
.IP
.EX
events := make(\f[B]chan\f[R] bashclient.PostCommandEvent, 100)
client.Observe.OnPostCommand(\f[B]func\f[R](e bashclient.PostCommandEvent) {
    \f[B]select\f[R] {
    \f[B]case\f[R] events <\- e:
    \f[B]default\f[R]: \f[I]// drop if full\f[R]
    }
})
\f[B]go\f[R] \f[B]func\f[R]() {
    \f[B]for\f[R] e := \f[B]range\f[R] events {
        \f[I]// heavy processing\f[R]
    }
}()
.EE
.SH Timeout handling
The Go binding does not impose a default timeout.
All request/response operations accept a \f[CR]context.Context\f[R]
parameter.
Use \f[CR]context.WithTimeout\f[R] or \f[CR]context.WithDeadline\f[R] to
control timeouts:
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// 10\-second timeout for eval\f[R]
ctx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(ctx, 10*time.Second)
\f[B]defer\f[R] cancel()
result, err := client.Eval(ctx, \(dqslow\-command\(dq)
\f[B]if\f[R] err != nil {
    \f[I]// may be context.DeadlineExceeded\f[R]
}

\f[I]// No timeout (blocks until response or client close)\f[R]
result, err := client.Eval(context.Background(), \(dqecho hello\(dq)
.EE
.PP
When the context expires, the pending select unblocks via the
context\(cqs \f[CR]Done()\f[R] channel, and a \f[CR]*TimeoutError\f[R]
wrapping \f[CR]context.DeadlineExceeded\f[R] is returned.
.SH Concurrency safety
The \f[CR]BashClient\f[R] is safe for concurrent use from multiple
goroutines with the following caveats:
.IP \(bu 2
Different channels can be used concurrently (each has its own response
channel).
.IP \(bu 2
Two goroutines calling the same channel concurrently may receive each
other\(cqs responses \(en avoid this pattern.
.IP \(bu 2
Callbacks run on the reader goroutine, so blocking in a callback stalls
all message dispatch.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]Close()\f[R] is safe to call from any goroutine and will unblock
any pending operations.
